{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "זה הדבר אשר צוה ה'", :translit "zeh ha-davar asher tzivah ha-shem", :gloss "this is the thing that YHWH commanded"} {:he "כל אלפ\"א ביתא", :translit "kol alef-bet", :gloss "the entire alef-bet"} {:he "כל המקיים את התורה", :translit "kol ha-mekayem et ha-torah", :gloss "all who fulfill the Torah"} {:he "מזמין לו ה' פרנסתו בלא טורח", :translit "mezamin lo ha-shem parnasato belo tireach", :gloss "HaShem prepares his sustenance for him without toil"} {:he "כאוכלי המן", :translit "ke-ochlei ha-man", :gloss "like the eaters of the manna"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Verse contains every letter of the alef-bet, indicating totality"}], :frame {:text "The manna is understood as a paradigm of effortless divine sustenance: Israel received it in the wilderness directly from Heaven (Exodus 16:4, 16:15). The Sages teach that Torah study is impossible without bread, linking sustenance to Torah engagement (Pirkei Avot 3:17). The Midrash identifies the manna as reward specifically given to those engaged in Torah, as it is written that manna fell 'so that I may test them whether they will walk in My Torah or not' (Exodus 16:4, expounded in Midrash Tanchuma, Beshalach 21).", :sources ["Exodus 16:4" "Exodus 16:15" "Exodus 16:16" "Pirkei Avot 3:17" "Midrash Tanchuma Beshalach 21"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Verse contains every letter of the alef-bet, indicating totality", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:16:37.990238213Z", :model "glm-5.2"}